  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (ja): ウサギアオイ",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%A6%E3%82%B5%E3%82%AE%E3%82%A2%E3%82%AA%E3%82%A4",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 110394537,
      "text": "ウサギアオイ は一年生あるいは多年生のハーブで、ヨーロッパ原産。北アフリカ、ヨーロッパ、アジア 由来だが、その他にも広範囲に帰化している。\n一般的には cheeseweed, cheeseweed mallow, Egyptian mallow, least mallow, little mallow, mallow, marshmallow, small-flowered mallow, small-flowered marshmallow あるいは smallflower mallow などと呼ばれている。和名は牧野富太郎による命名。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Malva parviflora",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malva_parviflora",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 1355518153,
      "text": "Malva parviflora is an annual or perennial herb that is native to Northern Africa, Southern Europe, and Western and Central Asia and is widely naturalized elsewhere. Common names include cheeseweed, cheeseweed mallow, Egyptian mallow, least mallow, little mallow, mallow, marshmallow, small-flowered mallow, small-flowered marshmallow, and smallflower mallow. It is used in traditional medicine."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Malva parviflora",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malva_parviflora",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 175438136,
      "text": "Malva parviflora es una especie de planta herbácea del género Malva, dentro de la familia Malvaceae. Es nativa de Asia, Europa y el norte de África, aunque introducida en América y la Macaronesia."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Malva parviflora",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malva_parviflora",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 237967218,
      "text": "Malva parviflora est une espèce de plantes à fleurs de la famille des Malvaceae. L'aire de répartition naturelle de cette espèce est la Macaronésie, de la Méditerranée au Pakistan et au Sahara. C'est une annuelle et elle pousse principalement dans le biome tempéré. Elle est utilisée pour traiter des troubles médicinaux non spécifiés, comme nourriture pour animaux, poison et médicament, elle a des usages environnementaux et pour l'alimentation."
    },
    "pt": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (pt): Malva parviflora",
      "source_url": "https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malva_parviflora",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 64612157,
      "text": "Malva parviflora é uma espécie de planta com flor pertencente à família Malvaceae."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "toxicity": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Malva parviflora § Toxicity",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malva_parviflora#Toxicity",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 1355518153,
        "heading": "Toxicity",
        "text": "M. parviflora accumulates pollutants such as cadmium and chromium. In nitrogen-rich soils, the plant's leaves and seeds can be toxic to cattle and poultry due to an accumulation of nitrates. If consumed in large amounts, the plant can damage energy homeostasis in horses, possibly due to its cyclopropene fatty acids.",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "fr": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Malva parviflora § Toxicité",
        "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malva_parviflora#Toxicit%C3%A9",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 237967218,
        "heading": "Toxicité",
        "text": "M. parviflora accumule des polluants tels que le cadmium et le chrome. Dans les sols riches en azote. Les feuilles et les graines de la plante peuvent être toxiques pour le bétail et la volaille en raison d'une accumulation de nitrates. Si elles sont consommées en grande quantité, la plante peut endommager l'homéostasie énergétique chez les chevaux, possiblement à cause de ses acides gras cyclopropènes.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
